<exercise class="RFM1">
  <problem>
    <title>Extending Your Thinking</title>
    
    <para>Using positive integers between 1 and 9 and each positive integer at most once, fill in values to get two constraints so that \(x=7\) is the only integer that will satisfy both constraints at the same time.
        ▢x + ▢ &lt;▢x + ▢
     </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>Your answers may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
    
    <para>\(4x + 1 &lt; 3x + 9\)</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--End interaction-->

<exercise class="RFM1">
  <problem>
    <para>Using positive integers between 1 and 9 and each positive integer at most once, fill in values to get two constraints so that \(x=7\) is the only integer that will satisfy both constraints at the same time.
        ▢x + ▢ &gt;▢x + ▢
     </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>Your answers may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
    
    <para>\(6x + 2 &gt; 5x + 8\)</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
